course: myths
lesson: 32
title: Siblings, cousins, grandparents — and the whole playground
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: At Christmas dinner Uncle Mike says "Bulls hate red!" for the tenth year running, and half the family groans. Family arguments are a special kind: nobody is going anywhere, and you'll all be at the table again next year.
explain: Big brothers are sometimes smug AND right; grandparents learned things long ago, often right then and still partly right now; little sisters need gentleness, not a lecture.
explain: The best move is to add to what someone knows instead of knocking it down: "You're right about grown-ups, Grandad, and babies actually have more bones!"
explain: Your big brother is right about blue whales but adds "you're only nine"; your best friend is still cross after you were bossy in a row about chameleons; and Uncle Mike's red-bull line is an old family joke everyone checked years ago, so now it is a tradition, not a fight.
explain: And in the playground, when five people laugh at one, the brave thing is to stand by the one, especially when he happens to be right.
fact: The blue whale is the largest animal known to have ever lived, heavier than any dinosaur found so far.
fact: Adults have about 206 bones; babies are born with around 270 to 300, and some fuse (join together) as they grow.
fact: The capital of Australia is Canberra, built as a compromise between Sydney and Melbourne (a deal where each side gives a bit, because both cities wanted to be the capital).
fact: Light travels nearly a million times faster than sound, which is why you see lightning before you hear thunder.
fact: Banana plants look like trees, but they are giant herbs; the "trunk" is made of tightly rolled leaves.
fact: Sugar and honey were used on wounds long ago, and doctors still use special medical-grade honey on some wounds, but at home a cut should just be cleaned and covered.
fact: Saturn is less dense (lighter for its size) than water, so in theory it would float, but no bath could ever hold it.
fact: There is no evidence that eating bananas before a match makes you score more goals; it is a team ritual.
fact: Bulls cannot tell red from green; in a bullfight, where a matador waves a cape at the bull, it is the cape's movement that makes them charge.
fact: Chameleons change colour mainly for mood, signalling and temperature.
fact: A dare like "say I'm the cleverest" turns a question into a fight about winning.
fact: When one person is laughed at by a group, standing by them takes courage and is kind.
exercise: Is the blue whale bigger than any dinosaur? | Yes, it's the largest animal known to have lived | No, lots of the big dinosaurs were far heavier still | No, the great white shark is bigger | Only if you count its tail
exercise: Your big brother is right about whales but says "you're only nine". What's a good reply? | You're right, but you don't have to say "only nine" | You're wrong, because you're mean | Nine-year-olds always know best, so I win the argument | Say nothing and sulk all day
exercise: How many bones do babies have compared with adults? | More, around 270 to 300, which fuse as they grow | Exactly the same, 206 | Fewer, because they're smaller | None, babies only have cartilage
exercise: Grandad says babies have 206 bones like adults. What's the kind way to correct him? | Agree about adults, then add that babies have more | Tell him old lessons are all wrong | Laugh and say he's out of date | Say nothing, because grandparents can't learn new things
exercise: What is the capital of Australia? | Canberra | Sydney | Melbourne | Perth
exercise: Why was Canberra chosen as the capital? | As a compromise between Sydney and Melbourne | Because it was the biggest city in the country | Because it's the closest city to Europe by ship | Because it was the very first city ever built there
exercise: Which is faster, light or sound? | Light, nearly a million times faster | Sound, which is why we hear thunder first | They travel at exactly the same speed | Sound, but only at night
exercise: Five kids laugh at Ben for saying light is faster than sound. What should you do? | Stand by Ben, he's right, and say not to pile on | Laugh along with them so they don't turn on you | Walk away and stay out of it | Tell Ben to stop saying silly things
exercise: What is a banana plant really? | A giant herb | A palm tree | A kind of bush | A tall grass
exercise: How should your little sister be corrected about banana trees? | Gently, sharing the weird fact with her | Loudly, so that she remembers it for ever | Not at all, because little ones can't learn | By laughing at her until she finally gets it
exercise: Nan says sugar helps a cut heal. What's true? | Doctors use special honey sometimes, but at home clean and cover a cut | Sugar from the kitchen heals any cut fastest | Sugar on a cut is the best first aid | Honey and sugar have never been used on wounds
exercise: Would Saturn float in a bath? | In theory, as it's less dense than water, but no bath is big enough | Yes, it would bob around in any bath | No, it's made of solid rock and iron | No, because it would melt the water
exercise: Do bananas before a match make you score more goals? | No, there's no evidence; it's a team ritual | Yes, every banana you eat means one more goal | Yes, but it only ever works for the strikers | No, bananas actually make you miss every shot
exercise: Why do bulls charge at the cape? | Because it moves | Because it's red | Because they smell the cloth | Because red makes them angry
exercise: Uncle Mike says "bulls hate red" for the tenth Christmas. What's a wise response? | Smile; the family already checked, and it's a tradition | Argue with him for a whole hour until he finally gives in | Refuse to come to Christmas | Shout that he's wrong every time
exercise: Your best friend is still cross after a row about chameleons. What helps most? | Say sorry for being bossy and start again | Explain all the chameleon facts once more, slowly | Wait until they admit that you were right all along | Find someone new to be your best friend instead
exercise: Why do chameleons mainly change colour? | Mood, signalling and temperature | Only to match their background | Because they're afraid of the dark | To copy the colour of their food
test: "lol saturn would float in a bath" one twin says; the other says "no way, it's a planet". Who's right? | Both a bit: it's less dense than water, but no bath could hold it | Only the second twin: planets sink | Only the first twin: it would float in our bath | Neither: Saturn is made of water
test: Cousin Leah, at the family quiz: "Sydney, obviously!" What's the friendly correction? | Lots of people think that; it's Canberra, built to stop an argument | Obviously not, are you thick? | It's Melbourne, everyone knows | Australia doesn't have a capital
test: Your cousin says, "If you're wrong you have to say I'm the cleverest person in the world." What's wise? | No dares, let's just look it up together | Accept the dare, because you're sure to win it | Make a bigger dare back | Give in and say it now
test: In a storm, you see a flash and hear the bang 5 seconds later. What does this show? | Light reaches you before sound does | Sound reaches you before light does | The flash and bang happen at different times | The storm is getting closer by itself
test: Your football team eats bananas before every match "for goals". What's sensible? | Let it go; rituals can be fun and bananas are a fine snack | Ban bananas because they're a myth | Tell the coach the team is stupid | Eat ten bananas to score ten goals
test: Grandad learned "206 bones" at school. Which part of what he learned is still right? | Adults have about 206 bones | Babies have exactly 206 bones | Bones never join together | Everyone has a different number
test: "sis thinks bananas grow on trees haha" What's the gentle, true thing to tell her? | They look like trees, but they're really giant herbs | Bananas grow under the ground, just like potatoes do | Bananas grow in bunches on bushes | Bananas are made in factories
test: Nan's mum put sugar on cuts. Your cousin says that's "total rubbish". What's fairer? | Honey has some real medical use, but at home clean and cover a cut | It's total rubbish, honey never helped anyone | Nan is right, sugar heals everything | Cuts should never be cleaned
test: What would happen if a matador waved a still red cape and a moving green one? | The bull would go for the moving green one | The bull would go for the still red one | The bull would ignore both | The bull would go for whichever is brighter red
test: Your brother is smug and right about blue whales. What's the honest and fair move? | Admit he's right, and ask him not to be mean about your age | Refuse to admit it because he's smug | Find a dinosaur picture to prove him wrong in front of everyone | Tell Mum he's lying
test: The whole playground is laughing at one kid. Which is the brave thing? | Stand with him and say not to pile on | Join in with them so the group likes you | Laugh quietly so that nobody notices you | Tell the teacher that he started it all
exam: Identify the animal regarded as the largest known to have existed. | The blue whale | Argentinosaurus | The African elephant | The whale shark
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the skeletal composition of the newborn infant. | It comprises more bones than the adult, certain of which subsequently fuse | It comprises precisely the same number as the adult | It comprises fewer bones than the adult | It comprises no bones whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the seat of government of the Commonwealth of Australia. | Canberra | Sydney | Melbourne | Brisbane
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a bull charges at the matador's cape. | The movement of the cape | The red colour of the cape | The odour of the cape | The sound of the cape
